INT. COMMUNITY GYM - EVENING

Fluorescent hum. A half-empty weight room. PRIYA OKAFOR, 17, wraps her hands at the mirror. COACH TOBIAS REEVE, 50s, stacks plates. In the corner, a HOODIE GUY sits on a bench, hood up, not lifting.

COACH REEVE
(to the room)
Ten minutes, people. Anyone who isn't warm in ten minutes is a spectator, and I don't coach spectators.

PRIYA
(under her breath)
Nobody's here but us.

She tightens the tape. The HOODIE GUY doesn't move.

PRIYA (CONT'D)
(to Reeve)
Coach, who is that? He's been sitting there since I came in.

COACH REEVE
Ask him.

PRIYA
I'm asking you.

COACH REEVE
(beat)
Then I'd say he's someone waiting for the right moment. Which, at his age, is the only kind of training that matters. You wait, you watch, you tell yourself you're not ready. And then one day the whole building is empty and you're still on the bench.

The front door SLAMS. Priya flinches. The HOODIE GUY stands.

HOODIE GUY
(pushing back his hood)
Priya. It's me.

PRIYA
(beat)
Desmond?

DESMOND
Two years, Pri. Two years and you didn't even look.

PRIYA
You told everyone you were moving. You told me you were done with all of this.

DESMOND
I said a lot of things.

A phone BUZZES on the bench. He ignores it.

PRIYA
(to the room, loud)
Does anyone else know about this? Coach? Because it feels like everyone knew but me.

COACH REEVE
(quietly)
I knew he'd come back. I didn't know when.

PRIYA
(muttering)
Unbelievable.

DESMOND
Say it to me. Whatever it is. I'd rather you hit me than go quiet.

Silence. A weight plate RATTLES on its stand.

PRIYA
(beat)
I was going to hit you. I practiced it. Every night I stood in front of that mirror and I practiced what I'd do, and now you're standing here with your stupid hood and I just want to know if you ate.

DESMOND
(laughing, wet-eyed)
I ate.

COACH REEVE
Right. Wraps on, both of you. Ten minutes.

Priya tosses Desmond a roll of tape. He catches it.
